"cuPQC-NTT: Number Theoretic Transform — cuPQC (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cupqc/libraries/cupqc_ntt/cupqc_ntt_feature.html)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cupqc/libraries/cupqc_ntt/cupqc_ntt_feature.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 118\nL0: Back to top\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search the docs ...] `Ctrl`+`K`\nL3: \nL4: Table of Contents\nL5: \nL6: Introduction\nL7: \nL8:   * 【0†Home】\nL9:   * 【1†Getting Started】\nL10:   * 【2†System Requirements】\nL11: \nL12: Libraries\nL13:   * 【3†cuPQC-BigInt】\nL14:     * 【4†Features】\nL15:     * 【5†Usage Guide】\nL16:     * 【6†API Reference】\nL17:       * 【7†Operators】\nL18:       * 【8†Types】\nL19:       * 【9†Device Functions】\nL20:   * 【10†cuPQC-NTT】\nL21:     * 【11†Features】\nL22:     * 【12†Usage Guide】\nL23:     * 【13†API Reference】\nL24:       * 【14†Operators】\nL25:       * 【15†Device Functions】\nL26:       * 【16†Host Functions】\nL27:       * 【17†Prime Field Constants】\nL28:       * 【18†Types】\nL29:   * 【19†cuPQC-Hash】\nL30:     * 【20†Features】\nL31:     * 【21†Usage Guide】\nL32:     * 【22†API Reference】\nL33:       * 【23†Hash Functions】\nL34:         * 【24†Operators】\nL35:         * 【25†Traits】\nL36:         * 【26†Device Functions】\nL37:       * 【27†Merkle Trees】\nL38:         * 【28†Operators】\nL39:         * 【29†Objects】\nL40:         * 【30†Device Functions】\nL41:   * 【31†cuPQC-PK】\nL42:     * 【32†Features】\nL43:     * 【33†Usage Guide】\nL44:     * 【34†API Reference】\nL45:       * 【35†Operators】\nL46:       * 【36†Traits】\nL47:       * 【37†Device Functions】\nL48:       * 【38†Host Functions】\nL49: Additional\nL50: \nL51:   * 【39†Release Notes】\nL52:   * 【40†Security Notes】\nL53:   * 【41†Software License Agreement】\nL54: # cuPQC-NTT: Number Theoretic Transform【42†#】\nL55: The cuPQC-NTT library provides GPU-optimized implementations of the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) designed for direct integration into CUDA kernels. The NTT is the modular-arithmetic analogue of the Fast Fourier Transform and is a core building block of lattice-based cryptography, zero-knowledge proof systems, and modular ring arithmetic over finite fields.\nL56: As a device-side library, cuPQC-NTT integrates directly into your CUDA kernels, enabling you to fuse NTTs with other GPU computations for maximum performance. Transforms run in the Montgomery domain; convert at load/store boundaries and convert twiddle tables once before reuse.\nL57: The notation in the panel below is used throughout the rest of this page for transform lengths and two-pass tiling.\nL58: \nL59: Notation for `N`, `M`, `K`, and `d`\nL60: \nL61: Transform length `N` is a power-of-two, with N =`2`^{`d`} and `d` = log_{2}(`N`).\nL62: \nL63: Sub-transform size `M` (a power-of-two) is set with `SubSize<M>`; `K = N/M`.\nL64: ## NTT Transform Modes【43†#】\nL65: \nL66: The cuPQC-NTT library provides two execution modes:\nL67: \nL68:   * Standard NTT: A complete `N`-point transform is performed in a single kernel launch per length-`N` buffer, with the full working set held in shared memory. This mode is ideal for transform lengths that fit within available shared memory.\nL69:   * Staged NTT: For large `N`, per-block shared memory cannot hold the full workspace for an `N`-point transform in a single pass, so a staged NTT is required. With `SubSize<M>` and two kernel passes: the first stage runs `M` parallel `K`-point contiguous NTTs (`K = N/M`); the second runs `K` parallel `M`-point strided NTTs. Each stage limits the per-block working set to `max(K, M)` elements, enabling transforms that would not fit in a single block’s shared memory.\nL70: ## Supported Configurations【44†#】\nL71: \nL72: The table below summarizes the supported Execution Modes, Transform Type, Prime Fields, and Precomputed Roots.\nL73: Aspect  | Summary\nL74: --- | ---\nL75: Execution modes  | Standard: One-kernel transform of length-`N`. Staged: Two-kernel transforms of `(N, SubSize<M>)` pairs.\nL76: Transform type  | Cyclic: Length-`N` transforms for arithmetic in the cyclic polynomial ring (modulo `x^{N} - 1`).\nL77: Prime field  | Custom: User-selected prime `p` < `2`^{`62`}. Built-in: `BabyBear` / `KoalaBear`.\nL78: Precomputed roots  | `BabyBear` / `KoalaBear`: `*_primitive_root_S` (`10` ≤ `S` ≤ `24`).\nL79: See 【17†Prime Field Constants】 for built-in prime values and compile-time root symbols.\nL80: \nL81: Both Standard NTT and Staged NTT support `uint16_t`, `uint32_t`, and `uint64_t` as precisions for any prime modulus. The next two subsections tabulate supported transform-size limits for Standard NTT and Staged NTT.\nL82: ### Standard NTT Limits【45†#】\nL83: \nL84: The table lists Standard NTT (single-kernel) limits on transform length `N` and exponent `d`. Coefficients and the modulus use the same type width (for example both `uint32_t`) for each prime and coefficient-width row.\nL85: \nL86: Prime  | Width (precision)  | Transform Size (N =`2`^{`d`})\nL87: --- | --- | ---\nL88: Custom `p`  | `uint16_t`  | `2 ≤ d ≤ 14`\nL89: `uint32_t`  | `2 ≤ d ≤ 13`\nL90: `uint64_t`  | `2 ≤ d ≤ 13`\nL91: ### Staged NTT Limits【46†#】\nL92: \nL93: The table lists Staged NTT (two-kernel) limits, pairing each supported transform length `N` with allowed `SubSize<M>` values.\nL94: \nL95: Transform Size (N =`2`^{`d`})  | Allowed Sub-transform Size (`SubSize<M>`)\nL96: --- | ---\nL97: `14 ≤ d ≤ 21`  | `256` · `512` · `1024` · `2048` · `4096` · `8192`\nL98: `d = 22`  | `512` · `1024` · `2048` · `4096` · `8192`\nL99: `d = 23`  | `1024` · `2048` · `4096` · `8192`\nL100: `d = 24`  | `2048` · `4096` · `8192`\nL101: ## Key Features【47†#】\nL102: \nL103:   * High Performance: GPU-optimized NTT kernels exploit shared memory and warp-level parallelism for maximum throughput.\nL104: \nL105:   * Montgomery Domain: Device transforms operate on Montgomery-domain data, with conversion at load/store (`load_to_mont` / `store_from_mont` and staged counterparts); twiddle tables are converted once before reuse.\nL106: \nL107:   * Cyclic Transforms: Implements the cyclic NTT for rings modulo `x^{N} - 1`.\nL108:   * Flexible Field Support: Supports user-selected primes up to 62-bit and built-in primes `BabyBear` / `KoalaBear`.\nL109: \nL110:   * Staged Transforms: The `SubSize<M>` operator enables memory-efficient transforms at large `N` by decomposing them into two kernel passes.\nL111: \nL112:   * Reusable Twiddle Tables: Twiddle factors are computed once and reused across an entire batch, minimizing startup overhead.\nL113: \nL114: On this page\nL115: 【48†Privacy Policy†www.nvidia.com】 | 【49†Your Privacy Choices†www.nvidia.com】 | 【50†Terms of Service†www.nvidia.com】 | 【51†Accessibility†www.nvidia.com】 | 【52†Corporate Policies†www.nvidia.com】 | 【53†Product Security†www.nvidia.com】 | 【54†Contact†www.nvidia.com】\nL116: \nL117: Copyright © 2024-2026, NVIDIA Corporation & Affiliates. All rights reserved.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGPU-Accelerated Big Integer Arithmetic and Number Theoretic Transform for Cryptography in NVIDIA cuPQC 0.6 - cuPQC SDK Developer Hub (https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 795\nL0: [Input] [Input]\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Skip to content】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Image: NVIDIA】 【2†Image: NVIDIA】 cuPQC SDK Developer Hub\nL5: \nL6: 【3†Home】 【4†Get Started】 【5†Applications】 【6†Blog】 【7†Docs】\nL7: \nL8: [Input] [Input]\nL9: \nL10: 【8†SDK v0.6.0†developer.nvidia.com】 【9†Sample Applications†github.com】\nL11: \nL12: [Input] 【3†Home】 【4†Get Started】 【5†Applications】 【6†Blog】 【7†Docs】\nL13: \nL14: 【8†SDK v0.6.0†developer.nvidia.com】 【9†Sample Applications†github.com】\nL15: \nL16: Categories\nL17: \nL18: 【6†All posts7】 【10†Release Blog3】 【11†Technical Blog2】 【12†Ecosystem2】\nL19: \nL20: Archive\nL21: 【6†All years7】 【13†20265】 【14†20251】 【15†20241】\nL22: # GPU-Accelerated Big Integer Arithmetic and Number Theoretic Transform for Cryptography in NVIDIA cuPQC 0.6\nL23: \nL24: August 28, 2026 cuPQC SDK v0.6\nL25: We're excited to announce cuPQC 0.6, delivering Big Integer Arithmetic (BigInt) and the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) as foundational math layer primitives specifically built for cryptographic application developers working on NVIDIA GPUs. These libraries are engineered to power the entire cryptographic spectrum, from classical protocols and post-quantum cryptography (PQC) to the massive computational workloads at the core of Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKPs) and Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE).\nL26: ## What's New in 0.6\nL27: Whether you are scaling traditional public-key cryptography like RSA and ECC, implementing post-quantum schemes, or bringing advanced privacy-preserving technologies like ZKPs and FHE to production, you face the same bottleneck: executing millions of multi-precision integer operations and polynomial multiplications at high throughput.\nL28: Historically, accelerating these workloads on GPUs meant implementing and optimizing custom CUDA kernels from scratch for every specific ring dimension, bit width, and parameter set your schemes require.\nL29: cuPQC 0.6 eliminates this barrier by providing that arithmetic through the cuPQC-BigInt and cuPQC-NTT libraries as ready-to-use GPU primitives. You can now compose complex cryptographic protocols using accelerated multi-precision integer arithmetic and polynomial multiplication as standard building blocks.\nL30: These libraries are designed as device extensions, enabling you to fuse these arithmetic operations with your own specialized functions into a single kernel. This avoids unnecessary host-device data transfers and intermediate memory allocations, a critical requirement for applications like ZKP proving, FHE ciphertext evaluation, and large-scale public-key infrastructure.\nL31: Batch-oriented APIs enable thousands of concurrent operations to execute in parallel, letting you build faster, more scalable cryptographic solutions while focusing on your protocol design rather than arithmetic implementation.\nL32: This release adds:\nL33:   * cuPQC-BigInt: Provides fixed-width unsigned integers with full arithmetic up to 4096 bits and storage up to 8192 bits, with thread and warp execution models, Barrett and Montgomery reduction paths, and compile-time error-handling policies. It serves as the workhorse for classical cryptography, the classical halves of hybrid PQC protocols, and large-field ZKP arithmetic.\nL34:   * cuPQC-NTT: Provides cyclic forward and inverse transforms in the Montgomery domain, in both standard and staged execution modes, for transform lengths up to 2^{24} points over a user-selected prime up to 62 bits. This provides the exact heavy lifting required for the massive polynomial rings used in FHE and ZKP systems.\nL35: In our benchmarks, cuPQC-NTT runs 32-bit transforms 12× to 42× faster than a 28-core CPU across sizes from 2^{8} to 2^{24} points, and cuPQC-BigInt runs 34× to 773× faster than a single CPU core running GMP across the eight operations we measured.\nL36: These additions expand the cuPQC SDK into four libraries across two tiers. At the math layer, cuPQC-BigInt and cuPQC-NTT provide low-level arithmetic, while cuPQC-Hash delivers key ZKP primitives like Poseidon2 and Merkle trees. Together, they give developers full flexibility to either deploy ready-to-use algorithms or construct custom, high-performance cryptographic pipelines on GPUs.\nL37: The following sections walk through each library in detail, covering supported operations, key configuration parameters, and benchmark results.\nL38: ## cuPQC-BigInt: Multi-Precision Integer Arithmetic\nL39: Modern cryptographic algorithms work with numbers that are orders of magnitude larger than what standard processor types can handle natively. Zero-Knowledge Proofs operate over 256- to 384-bit scalar fields, ECC works over 256- to 512-bit prime fields, post-quantum isogeny schemes use primes in the 400- to 700-bit range, and traditional RSA keys span up to 4096 bits.\nL40: In implementations, these values are held in multi-precision representations, with modular arithmetic applied at full width throughout the protocol. When thousands of these operations must run concurrently at high throughput, carrying the load on CPUs alone quickly becomes an expensive bottleneck.\nL41: cuPQC-BigInt provides these values as fixed-width unsigned integers built from 32-bit little-endian limbs. Widths are multiples of 32. Full arithmetic is available up to 4096 bits, and the double-width type used for products stores up to 8192 bits. This single library covers standard key sizes, elliptic curve fields, and large ZKP proof-system fields without requiring a separate implementation for each.\nL42: The specific widths you can instantiate depend on how many threads cooperate on a single integer, as detailed in Table 2. Just like the native integer types they extend, arithmetic wraps modulo 2^{bit_width}.\nL43: A full product of two N-bit values is up to 2N bits, so `mul_wide` and `square` return a separate double-width type carrying twice the limbs, complete with `lo` and `hi` accessors for its halves. At the 4096-bit arithmetic limit that product is 8192 bits. That wide value is also what Barrett and Montgomery reductions take as input to reduce it back to a single width.\nL44: When you only need part of a product, `mul_low` and `mul_high` return a single width directly, and `mul_low` costs roughly half as much as a full wide multiply.\nL45: ### Operations\nL46: \nL47: The table below is a high-level view of the functions and operators. Overloads, execution-model restrictions, and full signatures are in 【16†Supported Operations†docs.nvidia.com】 in the cuPQC-BigInt Features guide.\nL48: \nL49: cuPQC-BigInt Operations\nL50: \nL51: `name()` = callable · `+` = operator\nL52: \nL53: Capability Functions and operators\nL54: \nL55:   * 01\nL56: \nL57: Construction and access\nL58: \nL59: Build a value from scalars or limbs, read parts of it back, and write it to global memory.\nL60: \nL61: `bigint()`from a 32- or 64-bit scalar, or a limb array\nL62: `store()`whole value, or one instance of a batch\nL63: \nL64: `to_uint32()``to_uint64()`low bits of the value\nL65: \nL66: `[]`one limb, least significant first; thread execution only\nL67: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHigh-performance number theoretic transform on GPU through radix2-CT and 4-step algorithms - Sabanci University Research Database (https://research.sabanciuniv.edu/id/eprint/51889/)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://research.sabanciuniv.edu/id/eprint/51889/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 28\nL0: # High-performance number theoretic transform on GPU through radix2-CT and 4-step algorithms\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Image: Warning】  | The system is temporarily closed to updates for reporting purpose.\nL3: \nL4: Özcan, Ali Şah and Javeed, Arsalan and Savaş, Erkay (2025) High-performance number theoretic transform on GPU through radix2-CT and 4-step algorithms. IEEE Access, 13 . pp. 87862-87883. ISSN 2169-3536\nL5: PDF (Open Access (© 2025 The Authors.))\nL6: High-Performance.pdf\nL7: Available under License 【1†Creative Commons Attribution†creativecommons.org】.\nL8: 【2†Download (6MB)】\nL9: \nL10: Official URL: 【3†https://dx.doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3570024†dx.doi.org】\nL11: ## Abstract\nL12: The number theoretic transform (NTT) provides a practical and efficient technique to perform multiplication of very large degree polynomials typically found in fully homomorphic encryption (FHE), lattice-based cryptography, and non-interactive succinct zero-knowledge proof systems such as zk-SNARK. In this paper, we focus on this aspect and present two robust algorithms for efficient NTT using readily available GPU cards as hardware accelerators.\nL13: These algorithms are based on the radix-2 Cooley-Tukey (CT) and 4-Step techniques, which are rooted in classical FFT research. To this end, our algorithms leverage novel strategy to optimize memory access patterns adaptive to input size, which often is very large. Our approach: i) reduces and optimizes the number of accesses required for global memory for thread synchronization on the GPU device, and ii) systematically improves and enhances the use of spatial locality.\nL14: We achieve this effect by carefully controlling parameters such as the number of kernels, thread block size and shape, and thread layout, which directly impact overall NTT performance. The proposed optimizations enable our NTT implementation to handle very large polynomial sizes up to 228, which are usually a limiting factor in existing approaches, and achieve remarkable performance.\nL15: To the best of our knowledge, our proposed technique is unique and provides a recipe for selecting suitable configurable parameter combinations to achieve top performance for a given polynomial degree. Furthermore, we perform thorough experiments and empirically assess the performance of our proposed algorithms on three mainstream commercial GPU cards by NVIDIA.\nL16: Finally, we demonstrate that our algorithms compare favorably and outperform an existing commercial-grade open-source implementation in this arena.\nL17: Item Type:  | Article\nL18: Additional Information:  | This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License. For more information, see https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\nL19: Uncontrolled Keywords:  | Graphical Processing Unit; Hardware Acceleration; Homomorphic Cryptography; Number Theoretic Transform; Polynomial Arithmetic\nL20: Divisions:  | 【4†Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences】\nL21: Depositing User:  | 【5†Erkay Savaş】\nL22: Date Deposited:  | 19 Aug 2025 11:40\nL23: Last 